/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc7e4296f7f63ff65da5774d1aae950c8ee1d0df86ea0a304b0e58dccd5bec4a" "basis" 1521 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc7e4296f7f63ff65da5774d1aae950c8ee1d0df86ea0a304b0e58dccd5bec4a" "basis" 1521 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc7e4296f7f63ff65da5774d1aae950c8ee1d0df86ea0a304b0e58dccd5bec4a" "basis" 1521 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041802/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2-3\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2+3\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "1f45a9a7b9c087500becf0db14377b9adfd12e4aebff202abd821bdfa0616efc" "basis" 2008 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-38477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-3\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 9 & 3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+(3-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{1-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(1+\\text{i})}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38477" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16d7f531da0c66a4d6ecab3e3af806ab474aa5eb67d81c56a82870e9cf8082a0"} {"question" "q-38477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38477" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16d7f531da0c66a4d6ecab3e3af806ab474aa5eb67d81c56a82870e9cf8082a0"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{10}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{10}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-38494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16d7f531da0c66a4d6ecab3e3af806ab474aa5eb67d81c56a82870e9cf8082a0"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] ["math" "2+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16d7f531da0c66a4d6ecab3e3af806ab474aa5eb67d81c56a82870e9cf8082a0"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81031" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-(-5)(5) & =0 \\\\ -15-5\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(10)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 6\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+3\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-3\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16d7f531da0c66a4d6ecab3e3af806ab474aa5eb67d81c56a82870e9cf8082a0"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"} {"question" "q-81037" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073582/state.json" "file_sha256" "58fe3202ccdeb69f64ae1f4b646dbbcdece31c6ee5e190ed19bb359b66a2cb18"}] "usage" {"q-81037" [